Without the key, changing the name prop to Forest preserves the edited text River!. The state initializer is not a synchronization rule. With the key, the fresh instance starts with Forest.

after:
```react
function Draft({ name }) {
  const [text, setText] = React.useState(name);
  return (
    <section>
      <label>Draft <input value={text} onChange={e => setText(e.target.value)} /></label>
      <button onClick={() => setText(t => t + "!")}>Add mark</button>
    </section>
  );
}
function App() {
  const [name, setName] = React.useState("River");
  return (
    <main>
      <button onClick={() => setName(n => n === "River" ? "Forest" : "River")}>
        Switch topic
      </button>
      <Draft key={name} name={name} />
    </main>
  );
}
```
Add mark changes the draft to `River!`. Switch topic starts a fresh draft containing `Forest`.
